    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>
        <raoh.version>0.7.2</raoh.version>
        <jspecify.version>1.0.1</jspecify.version>
        <!-- Where compiled classes go, named so the `m2e` profile below can send the editor's
             somewhere else. A build outside the editor uses the Maven defaults. -->
        <build.classes>${project.basedir}/target/classes</build.classes>
        <build.test.classes>${project.basedir}/target/test-classes</build.test.classes>
        <errorprone.version>2.50.0</errorprone.version>
        <checkstyle.plugin.version>3.6.0</checkstyle.plugin.version>
        <!-- What the pre-commit hook hands to Checkstyle: the directory it wrote the staged files
             into. A build that is not the hook leaves it at the root module's sources, which do
             not exist, so the goal has nothing to read. -->
        <checkstyle.staged.dir>${project.basedir}/src/main/java</checkstyle.staged.dir>
        <checkstyle.version>14.0.0</checkstyle.version>
        <nullaway.version>0.14.0</nullaway.version>
        <!-- What NullAway is asked for, written once. The gate below and the `lint` profile both
             ask, and a gate configured differently in each is a gate whose answer depends on which
             build asked. -->
        <nullaway.args>-Xep:NullAway:ERROR -XepOpt:NullAway:OnlyNullMarked=true -XepOpt:NullAway:JSpecifyMode=true</nullaway.args>
        <!-- The nullness gate an ordinary build runs, carried by the modules that declare
             `@NullMarked` — souther-runtime, today. Nothing else in Error Prone: this is the one
             rule those modules state about themselves, and the other five hundred checks are the
             `lint` profile's.

             It is a property because the profile empties it. javac takes one `-Xplugin:ErrorProne`
             per compilation and reports the second as a plugin it cannot find, so under `lint`,
             which puts the plugin on every module, a module carrying its own would be handing javac
             two. Emptying this leaves the profile's the only one, and NullAway is in both spellings,
             so the gate does not lift when the profile takes over. -->
        <errorprone.gate>-Xplugin:ErrorProne -XepDisableAllChecks ${nullaway.args}</errorprone.gate>
        <!-- Every warning javac gives is an error. It is a property because the `lint` profile
             empties it: under that profile Error Prone's findings arrive as javac warnings too, and
             there are 889 of them — a backlog of its own, which this is not the thing to turn into
             a build failure. What this holds is javac's own warnings. -->
        <javac.werror>-Werror</javac.werror>
        <!-- The build protocol souther-build-driver implements. Not this project's version and not
             tracked to it: it moves when the interface between a build plugin and a driver moves,
             which is why it is released from souther-lang/souther-build-api and named here like any
             other outside dependency. -->
        <souther.build.api.version>1.1.0</souther.build.api.version>
        <junit.version>6.1.3</junit.version>
        <!-- Named rather than written where it is used: `souther init` writes a project that runs
             its tests, and what it hands that project is this number. -->
        <surefire.version>3.6.0</surefire.version>
        <!-- The coverage agent a run under `-Pcoverage-by-class` carries. Named once because two
             places need the same number: the profile that starts the agent and the test-support
             module that compiles against its runtime. -->
        <jacoco.version>0.8.14</jacoco.version>
        <!-- How many JVMs the tests are spread over. Named here so -DforkCount=N reaches it: a
             literal in the plugin's configuration outranks the property surefire declares, so the
             override the comment below offers was not one, and a fork count could not be measured
             until it was.

             A ceiling, and not a share of the machine. A share takes the whole of whatever it is
             run on, and what it is run on is usually a machine somebody is also working on: a fork
             brings its own compiler and collector threads, so a share of the cores is all of them
             once those are counted. Held to a share the suite finishes sooner and nothing else on
             the machine gets done while it runs, which is a trade the person waiting was never
             asked about.

             `NC` cannot say this. It is a multiplier, so the figure that gives this ceiling on a
             large machine gives less than one core on a small one, and a run on two cores would
             come down to a single JVM. The ceiling is written as itself, and a machine with fewer
             cores than this is told the number it has — which is what CI is passed. -->
        <forkCount>4</forkCount>
        <!-- How a fork compiles and collects. Named for the same reason the fork count is: a literal
             in the plugin's configuration is not overridable, and what these are worth has to be
             measurable against the suite as it stands rather than the one they were chosen on.
             The stack is not among them — it is written beside this one where the argument is
             built, because it is what this compiler is supported on rather than a way of running
             the tests faster, and a run measuring a collector would otherwise drop it. -->
        <test.jvm.args>-XX:TieredStopAtLevel=1 -XX:+UseSerialGC</test.jvm.args>
        <!-- The tag a test carries when its subjects are enumerated from what this repository
             carries rather than written in the test. Left out of a plain run and asked for by
             emptying this. -->
        <test.excluded.groups>population</test.excluded.groups>
        <!-- What a row of an example is held to while the suite runs. The shipped default is a
             hundred million, which is what a model somebody wrote deserves; the models here write a
             literal or a small record, and dozens of them hold a helper that loops on purpose,
             because what they are about is a row that does not come back. A looping row is walked
             to the limit before anything is reported about it, so the limit is what those tests
             cost: the compiler's suite is 140s under the shipped default and 73s under this one.
             Twenty times what `DoesNotComeBack` allows a row it expects to finish, so a model doing
             real work has room, and reached in milliseconds by one that loops. Named here so
             -Dtest.example.step.limit=N reaches it. -->
        <test.example.step.limit>1000000</test.example.step.limit>
    </properties>

